lesson k-a-ten-frame v=1 format=teach grade=0 unit=2 subject=maths levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Ten on a ten frame"
  goal "Count up to ten counters on a ten frame by seeing a full row as five, and say how many more make ten."

  look {
    scene 26x11 {
      tenframe frame count=7 color=mint at=canvas(1, 1)
      text note "Five and two more: seven." below=frame gap=1
    }
    say """
      A ten frame has two rows of five squares. When the top row is full, that is five. Seven is
      the full row and two more. When both rows are full, that is ten.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked k.frame-count n=7 level=medium
    }
    practice k.frame-count count=2 seed=3
    practice k.count-stars count=1 seed=4
    practice k.frame-empty count=2 seed=5
    practice k.frame-one-more count=2 seed=6
    practice k.which-frame count=1 seed=7
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show k.which-frame a=8 b=9 w=1 easy-a=6 easy-b=9 easy-w=0 hard-a=9 hard-b=10 hard-w=0
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show k.under-the-cup h=3 easy-h=2 hard-h=4
  }
  remember {
    say "A full row is five. Two full rows are ten."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Read each question aloud and let the child point and say the number. The frame is there so
    that seven is seen as five and two, not counted from one: if the child counts every counter,
    cover the top row with a hand and ask how many are under it. Off the page, an egg box with its
    lid cut off makes a ten frame, and dried pasta makes the counters. England, Reception, ELG
    Number: "Have a deep understanding of number to 10, including the composition of each number".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the frames hold five to seven, one frame is counted in the teacher's pen first, and the frames to compare differ by three or more."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the frames hold eight to ten, the empty squares are four or five, and the frames to compare differ by one."
  }
}
